{
  "slug": "next-permutation",
  "title": "Next Permutation",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "Two Pointers / suffix pivot swap",
  "statement": "Given an integer array `nums`, return the next permutation of `nums` in lexicographic order: the smallest rearrangement of the same multiset of values that is strictly greater than `nums` when compared element by element. Lexicographic comparison looks at the first index where the two arrays differ and prefers the smaller value at that index.\n\nIf no strictly greater rearrangement exists, that is when `nums` is already sorted in non-increasing order, return the same values sorted in non-decreasing order instead. Do not mutate the input array; return a new array.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 100` and `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000]",
      "output": "[-10000]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-221, 6635, 9954, -3483]",
      "output": "[-221, 9954, -3483, 6635]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Ask which suffix of the array can no longer be rearranged into anything larger: scan from the right until the values stop increasing.",
    "The element just before that suffix is the rightmost position that can still grow. Swap it with the smallest value to its right that is strictly bigger than it.",
    "After the swap the suffix is still in non-increasing order, so reversing it (not sorting it) yields the smallest possible tail."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force enumerates every way to change the array: for each pair of positions (i, j) with i < j it swaps those two entries, sorts everything after i into ascending order, throws the candidate away unless it is lexicographically greater than the input, and finally returns the smallest candidate that survived. That is O(n^2) candidates, each costing an O(n log n) rebuild and an O(n) comparison. The optimal solution reads the structure instead of enumerating. Walking left from the end, the longest suffix whose values never increase is already the largest arrangement those positions can form, so the answer must keep the prefix before it untouched and change things at the pivot, the single element sitting just before that suffix. Swapping the pivot with the smallest suffix value that still exceeds it makes the array greater at the earliest possible position; the suffix is still non-increasing after the swap, so reversing it turns it into ascending order, which is the smallest possible tail.",
    "why_optimal": "The correct answer has to agree with the input on the longest prefix that can stay unchanged, so any algorithm must locate that pivot, and locating it means inspecting the suffix at least once: Omega(n) reads are unavoidable. One right-to-left scan plus one swap plus one in-place reversal does exactly that in O(n) time, so the optimal implementation is within a constant factor of the lower bound. The output array itself is O(n), so the O(n) space line is the returned array, not working memory.",
    "pitfalls": "Comparing with > instead of >= during the pivot scan breaks duplicates: the longest non-increasing suffix must swallow equal neighbours, otherwise [1,5,1] style inputs return a larger permutation than asked. Swapping the pivot with any suffix element that is bigger, rather than with the smallest such element, produces something greater than the next permutation. Forgetting the no-pivot case (input already non-increasing) loses the wrap-around to ascending order. Reversing the suffix with a sort is correct but slower than a two-pointer reversal; reversing only part of the suffix wastes the non-increasing invariant. Finally, sorting nums in place to build the fallback answer mutates the caller's array."
  },
